WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.820
Hey, welcome to SpaceX Daily, episode 26.

00:00:03.220 --> 00:00:06.560
It's July 8th, 2026, and I'm Patrick in Vancouver.

00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:09.200
From the launch pad to the trading floor,

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:10.920
here's everything that moved today.

00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:13.660
Starship, lunar ride share contracts

00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:17.240
move orbital refueling and heavy payload delivery timelines

00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:20.000
forward by demonstrating early commercial demand.

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:22.840
ISpace has booked a starship slot

00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:25.400
to offer lunar ride share cargo services

00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:27.800
at lower cost than dedicated landers.

00:00:28.520 --> 00:00:30.440
The agreement targets multiple missions

00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:32.860
that leveraged starships payload capacity

00:00:32.860 --> 00:00:35.980
for secondary payloads alongside primary cargo.

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:39.660
Remember, the show covered Starship's flight test arc

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:41.000
on yesterday's episode.

00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:44.560
This development fits by adding commercial lunar demand

00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:47.560
that could accelerate orbital refueling demonstrations

00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:51.540
planned for the 2026 to 2027 window.

00:00:52.180 --> 00:00:55.640
The first manifested ISpace payload integration timeline

00:00:55.640 --> 00:00:57.880
is now the near-term item to track.

00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:03.020
An FAA advisory sets a not earlier than date of July 14

00:01:03.020 --> 00:01:05.860
for Starship's next integrated flight test.

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:08.780
The window aligns with ongoing booster

00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:10.720
and ship processing at starbase.

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:13.760
This test remains the next data point

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:16.560
in the campaign aimed at demonstrating booster catch

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:17.820
and rapid reuse.

00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:21.940
Any slip would ripple directly into the refueling timeline.

00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:24.280
The ISpace agreement now depends on.

00:01:25.160 --> 00:01:29.000
SpaceX filed an application to operate up to 100,000

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:30.940
next-generation starlink satellites

00:01:30.940 --> 00:01:34.380
optimized for higher capacity AI era traffic.

00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:38.720
The Gen3 design targets expanded laser link networking

00:01:38.720 --> 00:01:40.540
and direct to cell backhaul.

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:44.460
Recall that starlink expansion was discussed yesterday.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:47.960
This request continues the constellation growth arc

00:01:47.960 --> 00:01:51.420
and directly tests subscriber and data demand scaling.

00:01:52.060 --> 00:01:54.340
Regulators response timeline will determine

00:01:54.340 --> 00:01:57.080
when additional orbital shells can be populated.

00:01:57.500 --> 00:02:00.760
SpaceX continues to secure additional national security

00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:03.980
launch and satellite contracts across multiple agencies.

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:07.060
The awards build on existing star shield

00:02:07.060 --> 00:02:09.400
and launch cadence work without altering

00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:10.920
current manifest priorities.

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:13.820
These contracts directly support Falcon

00:02:13.820 --> 00:02:16.620
and future Starship national security missions.

00:02:17.240 --> 00:02:19.920
Watch for the next round of Space Force task orders

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:21.900
that could affect booster assignment.

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:26.220
Falcon 9 successfully deployed the transporter 17 mission

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:29.280
on July 7th carrying multiple small satellites.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.900
The flight continues the dedicated ride share cadence

00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:35.560
that has become a core part of Falcon operations.

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:39.780
Industry voices cited in the report expressed uncertainty

00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:42.680
over long-term availability of similar slots.

00:02:43.340 --> 00:02:46.200
Observers will track whether future transporter missions

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:48.300
maintain the same manifest density.

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:52.140
Apelink confirmed contact with its initial satellite

00:02:52.140 --> 00:02:54.860
launched on the July 7th ride share mission.

00:02:55.460 --> 00:02:58.480
The spacecraft will demonstrate data relay capability

00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:01.240
under an experimental FCC license.

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:04.800
The successful checkout keeps the ride share program

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:06.880
secondary payload model intact.

00:03:07.380 --> 00:03:10.400
Next milestone is the planned relay demonstration pass.

00:03:11.380 --> 00:03:13.560
Apelink's first satellite checkout occurred

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:15.520
after the July 7 launch.

00:03:16.360 --> 00:03:19.880
The experimental license covers the initial data relay tests.

00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:22.180
Observers note the checkout supports

00:03:22.180 --> 00:03:24.940
continued secondary payload flights on Falcon.

00:03:25.820 --> 00:03:29.580
Reddit users are tracking a recent NSF space-coast flyover

00:03:29.580 --> 00:03:32.520
showing expanded Florida ground support infrastructure

00:03:32.520 --> 00:03:35.220
and multiple Starship-related road closures.

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:39.140
Observers note visible progress on new tank farms

00:03:39.140 --> 00:03:41.340
and transport routes near existing pads.

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:44.940
A separate thread discusses whether controlled satellite

00:03:44.940 --> 00:03:48.660
d-orbits inside Starship could serve as a proving ground

00:03:48.660 --> 00:03:51.020
for closed loop material recycling.

00:03:51.660 --> 00:03:53.780
Noting that recovering hardware intact

00:03:53.780 --> 00:03:57.280
would reduce both orbital debris and raw material demand

00:03:57.280 --> 00:03:58.300
for future builds.

00:03:59.180 --> 00:04:02.540
A separate post examines how Starships larger diameter

00:04:02.540 --> 00:04:04.920
might affect ground transport logistics

00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:08.000
once the vehicle moves beyond current test articles.

00:04:08.680 --> 00:04:11.980
The diameter change raises questions about route clearances

00:04:11.980 --> 00:04:13.660
and support equipment sizing.

00:04:14.340 --> 00:04:16.400
Participants note that wider sections

00:04:16.400 --> 00:04:19.260
could require new overland transport fixtures.

00:04:19.820 --> 00:04:21.980
The thread ties these logistics questions

00:04:21.980 --> 00:04:24.800
to future star-based to Florida movement plans.

00:04:25.420 --> 00:04:28.420
Worth keeping an eye on, industry executives quoted

00:04:28.420 --> 00:04:31.540
in coverage of the Transporter 17 launch described

00:04:31.540 --> 00:04:34.580
growing concern that the dedicated ride share cadence

00:04:34.580 --> 00:04:37.280
may not remain available at current frequency.

00:04:37.880 --> 00:04:40.160
The worry centers on manifest pressure

00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:42.620
from Starlink and national security missions

00:04:42.620 --> 00:04:45.440
that could crowd out third party small satellites.

00:04:46.220 --> 00:04:49.520
Resolution would require either additional Falcon vehicles

00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:52.080
or a clear Starship ride share pathway

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:53.620
that preserves slot access.

00:04:54.600 --> 00:04:56.980
The May 28 blue origin incident

00:04:56.980 --> 00:04:59.680
at Cape Canaveral's Launch Complex 36

00:04:59.680 --> 00:05:02.940
has prompted federal review of launch site capacity.

00:05:03.620 --> 00:05:06.560
Options under discussion include new pad construction,

00:05:07.220 --> 00:05:09.900
shared use agreements and regulatory streamlining.

00:05:10.740 --> 00:05:12.900
The review directly affects future Falcon

00:05:12.900 --> 00:05:16.200
and Starship manifest flexibility at Florida ranges.

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:19.960
Any policy change would influence pad turnaround times

00:05:19.960 --> 00:05:21.620
and annual launch ceilings.

00:05:22.340 --> 00:05:25.380
Federal officials are evaluating infrastructure upgrades

00:05:25.380 --> 00:05:27.180
at existing Florida facilities.

00:05:27.880 --> 00:05:29.900
The review process includes input

00:05:29.900 --> 00:05:33.480
from multiple launch providers operating at Cape Canaveral.

00:05:33.880 --> 00:05:36.980
A German firm has secured a decade-long lease

00:05:36.980 --> 00:05:39.880
on a Nova Scotia launch pad for sounding rocket

00:05:39.880 --> 00:05:41.620
and small launcher operations.

00:05:42.100 --> 00:05:44.840
The agreement adds a new North American site

00:05:44.840 --> 00:05:46.920
outside traditional US ranges.

00:05:47.640 --> 00:05:51.640
It does not directly affect space X Florida or Texas pads,

00:05:51.900 --> 00:05:53.320
but illustrates growing demand

00:05:53.320 --> 00:05:55.480
for dedicated launch infrastructure.

00:05:56.200 --> 00:05:58.440
Watch for any follow-on commercial agreements

00:05:58.440 --> 00:05:59.620
at the same facility.

00:06:00.200 --> 00:06:02.640
The 10-year lease covers both sounding rockets

00:06:02.640 --> 00:06:04.300
and small orbital vehicles.

00:06:05.180 --> 00:06:07.320
Nova Scotia officials highlighted the site

00:06:07.320 --> 00:06:09.880
as an expansion of regional space activity.

00:06:10.380 --> 00:06:16.300
On the AI front, SpaceX's FCC filing for 100,000 Gen3 satellites

00:06:16.300 --> 00:06:18.560
explicitly frames the constellation

00:06:18.560 --> 00:06:20.940
as an AI-era global backbone,

00:06:21.660 --> 00:06:23.400
citing higher laser-link throughput

00:06:23.400 --> 00:06:25.440
and direct-to-cell capacity needed

00:06:25.440 --> 00:06:27.800
for large-scale model inference traffic.

00:06:28.200 --> 00:06:31.980
The application does not detail on orbit processing hardware

00:06:31.980 --> 00:06:34.260
but ties orbital expansion directly

00:06:34.260 --> 00:06:37.600
to anticipated data center and edge compute demand.

00:06:38.160 --> 00:06:39.900
From an engineering standpoint,

00:06:40.340 --> 00:06:43.140
Starship's ride share architecture for lunar cargo

00:06:43.140 --> 00:06:45.720
rests on a simple mass fraction advantage.

00:06:46.420 --> 00:06:48.660
Once the vehicle reaches low Earth orbit,

00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:50.400
its remaining propellant budget

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:52.840
can deliver several times the payload mass

00:06:52.840 --> 00:06:54.360
of a dedicated lander

00:06:54.900 --> 00:06:56.980
because the same booster and tanker fleet

00:06:56.980 --> 00:06:58.820
already exist for other missions.

00:06:59.580 --> 00:07:02.880
The key design choice is treating the Starship upper stage

00:07:02.880 --> 00:07:04.980
as a reusable transfer vehicle

00:07:04.980 --> 00:07:07.100
rather than a single-use lander

00:07:07.660 --> 00:07:10.180
which collapses the per kilogram cost floor

00:07:10.180 --> 00:07:12.820
to the marginal expense of extra propellant

00:07:12.820 --> 00:07:14.540
and one additional tanker flight.

00:07:15.040 --> 00:07:17.840
That approach only works if orbital refueling

00:07:17.840 --> 00:07:21.200
can be performed reliably within the same flight test cadence

00:07:21.200 --> 00:07:25.080
already planned for 2026 to 2027.

00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:28.780
If the first successful refueling demonstration slips,

00:07:29.220 --> 00:07:31.200
the lunar ride share value proposition

00:07:31.200 --> 00:07:33.720
shrinks back toward the higher fixed cost

00:07:33.720 --> 00:07:35.580
of building dedicated landers.

00:07:36.200 --> 00:07:38.480
The next measurable milestone is therefore

00:07:38.480 --> 00:07:40.660
not the e-space contract itself

00:07:40.660 --> 00:07:43.600
but the first on-orbit propellant transfer test

00:07:43.600 --> 00:07:46.020
that validates the underlying mass budget.

00:07:46.660 --> 00:07:52.120
And a quick market note, SPCX is at $149.47

00:07:52.120 --> 00:07:57.440
down 1%, before we go, the July 14 flight test window

00:07:57.440 --> 00:08:00.280
will show whether refueling hardware stays on schedule

00:08:00.280 --> 00:08:02.460
for the lunar missions now pulling demand.

00:08:03.140 --> 00:08:05.760
And that's a wrap on today's SpaceX developments.

00:08:06.780 --> 00:08:11.440
SPCX is trading at $149.47 down 1%.

